      "message": "I\u0027ve thought about this a bit more and I think we can be smarter than this. The `payload_to_info` function feels like a bad idea in the end. The issue we have is \"we receive a packet carrying info as its payload, but scapy steps in and due to how protocol parsing works, it can take some bytes and reinterpret them\". But we already know per-test that those bytes are really just our test info. So I\u0027m thinking - let\u0027s forget the udp-specific function and instead rework the whole logic. Drop `payload_to_info` completely, introduce `get_info_from_packet` taking a  packet as a parameter instead of payload. This effectively reverts the previous change, but in the end we get a \"just works\" function instead of having to remember to use A or B based on circumstances.\n\nWhat do you think?",
